WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
How do you measure metabolism inside a body without opening it?

2
00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:10.540
Every living system trades oxygen, carbon dioxide, and heat with its surroundings.

3
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:15.360
Seal it in a chamber, and those flows expose the hidden engine.

4
00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:21.190
Start with oxygen, a method called respirometry.

5
00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:25.190
Compare air entering a lung with air leaving.

6
00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:32.230
Air enters at about 21% % oxygen and leaves at about 16%.

7
00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:38.390
21 minus 16 leaves about 5% % of its volume taken up as oxygen.

8
00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:43.190
Multiply that drop by the air breathed to get oxygen use.

9
00:00:44.283 --> 00:00:46.363
Now follow heat: calorimetry.

10
00:00:47.783 --> 00:00:53.243
Metabolic rate is energy used per unit time, in watts, and nearly all becomes heat.

11
00:00:54.563 --> 00:00:58.743
At steady state, body temperature holds constant, so no heat is stored.

12
00:01:00.063 --> 00:01:03.543
Heat made plus heat gained equals dry leak plus evaporation.

13
00:01:04.463 --> 00:01:08.043
Dry leak means radiation, contact and moving air.

14
00:01:09.117 --> 00:01:12.517
With nothing heating the animal, heat made equals heat lost.

15
00:01:13.857 --> 00:01:18.557
Burning fuel also uses oxygen roughly in step with the energy released.

16
00:01:19.877 --> 00:01:24.357
So at steady state, oxygen used and heat lost reveal metabolic rate.

17
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:29.600
Carbon dioxide adds a second clue, but one pathway tests it.

18
00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:36.560
In the pentose phosphate pathway, one step releases carbon dioxide without using any

19
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:43.740
oxygen. Predict: does running that pathway raise the whole body's CO₂/O₂ ratio?

20
00:01:49.483 --> 00:01:55.943
Surprisingly, no. That step also makes NADPH, an electron carrier.

21
00:01:57.543 --> 00:02:01.483
Cells later spend it on oxygen-using reactions or on making fat.

22
00:02:02.743 --> 00:02:07.983
So the pathway alone does not raise the body's CO₂/O₂ ratio.

23
00:02:09.403 --> 00:02:14.263
To measure that pathway, experimenters use tracers that follow glucose's first

24
00:02:14.263 --> 00:02:16.483
carbon, the one this step releases.

25
00:02:17.767 --> 00:02:19.287
Chambers need controls.

26
00:02:20.807 --> 00:02:25.847
First, wait for steady state, when flows and body temperature hold constant and no

27
00:02:25.847 --> 00:02:32.167
heat is stored. Second, hold air and walls at the same temperature, so dry heat

28
00:02:32.167 --> 00:02:33.867
leaks toward one known temperature.

29
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.360
Oxygen records unlocked landmarks.

30
00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:43.700
Szent-Györgyi and then Krebs measured oxygen use in minced pigeon breast muscle.

31
00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:50.660
A pinch of four-carbon acid made the muscle burn far more oxygen than oxidizing it

32
00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:56.960
could explain. The acid was not spent as fuel; it was reused to burn other fuel,

33
00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.140
revealing the citric acid cycle.

34
00:03:00.233 --> 00:03:05.793
An oxygen electrode tracks the oxygen left around isolated mitochondria.

35
00:03:07.073 --> 00:03:11.733
Adding ADP, spent ATP, steepened the slow oxygen line: State 3.

36
00:03:13.013 --> 00:03:16.533
Once the ADP became ATP, it flattened: State 4.

37
00:03:17.693 --> 00:03:23.733
The respiratory chain burns the oxygen but never touches ADP; ATP synthase does.

38
00:03:24.817 --> 00:03:29.937
In 1961, Peter Mitchell proposed that a proton gradient links the two.

39
00:03:31.237 --> 00:03:36.117
From whole mammals to isolated mitochondria, measure what crosses a controlled

40
00:03:36.117 --> 00:03:39.377
boundary, and hidden metabolism comes into view.
